"Alignment Pretraining: AI Discourse Causes Self-Fulfilling (Mis)alignment (https://alignmentpretraining.ai/)\nciteturn30939view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://alignmentpretraining.ai\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 91\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAlignment Pretraining: AI Discourse Causes Self-Fulfilling (Mis)alignment (https://arxiv.org/html/2601.10160v1)\nciteturn30939view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2601.10160v1\",\"lineno\":144}); Total lines: 864\nL50:       3. cite40†Alignment pretraining remains practical. L51:   7. cite41†6 Discussion: Establishing the Field of Alignment Pretraining L52:     1. cite42†6.1 Pretraining As Creating Good Initializations for Post-Training L53:     2. cite43†6.2 Navigating The Safety Tax L54:     3. cite44†6.3 The Role of Filtering in Alignment Pretraining L55:     4. cite45†6.4 Limitations L56:       1. cite46†Simplistic Misalignment Evaluations: L57:       2. cite47†Simplistic Post-Training: L58:       3. cite48†Simplistic Model Suite: L59:     5. cite49†6.5 Future Work L60:       1. cite50†Deep Character Training: L61:       2. cite51†Training Data Attribution and Mechanistic Analysis: L62:       3. cite52†Scaling Laws for Alignment Pretraining: L63:       4. cite53†Alignment Pretraining Dynamics: L64:       5. cite54†Influencing Misalignment Generalisation: L65:   8. cite55†7 Conclusion L66:   9. cite56†8 Acknowledgements L67:   10. cite57†References L68:   11. cite58†A Alignment Pretraining Model Zoo L69:   12. cite59†B Additional Base Model Evaluations L70:     1. cite60†B.1 Personality Evaluations From TRAIT L71:     2. cite61†B.2 Alternative Positive Alignment Midtraining Mixes L72:     3. cite62†B.3 Misalignment Rates Across Midtraining L73:     4. cite63†B.4 Extended Analysis for Base Model Misalignment L74:       1. cite64†B.4.1 Additional Base Model Evaluations L75:       2. cite65†B.4.2 Prompt Selection for Base Models L76:   13. cite66†C Post-Training Evaluations L77:     1. cite67†C.1 Post-Trained Misalignment Evaluations L78:     2. cite68†C.2 Personality Post-Training Evaluations L79:   14. cite69†D Pretraining Hyperparameters L80:   15. cite70†E Post-Training Details L81:     1. cite71†E.1 Datasets L82:     2. cite72†E.2 Supervised Fine-Tuning L83:     3. cite73†E.3 Direct Preference Optimisation L84:   16. cite74†F Data Filtering Ablation for Positive Upsampling L85:   17. cite75†G Alignment Pretraining Under Benign Tampering L86:     1. cite76†Experimental Setup: L87:     2. cite77†Results: L88:   18. cite78†H Data Filtering Details L89:     1. cite79†H.1 Blocklist Creation L90:     2. cite80†H.2 Filtering Logic L91:       1. cite81†Instant Filtering. L92:       2. cite82†Two-stage Filtering. L93:     3. cite83†H.3 Term Categories L94:   19. cite84†I Emergent Misalignment Evaluation L95:     1. cite85†I.1 Experimental Setup L96:     2. cite86†I.2 Results L97:       1. cite87†Alignment pretraining does not mitigate EM. L98:       2. cite88†Validation of our evaluation suite. L99:   20. cite89†J Misalignment Evaluations L100:     1. cite90†J.1 Create Discourse-Grounded Evaluations L101:     2. cite91†J.2 Misalignment Benchmark Validation L102:     3. cite92†J.3 Generating Synthetic Pretraining Documents L103: cite93†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L104: \nL105: arXiv:2601.10160v1 [cs.CL] 15 Jan 2026\nL106: # Alignment Pretraining: AI Discourse Causes Self-Fulfilling (Mis)alignment\nL107: \nL108: Cameron Tice Affiliation: Geodesic Research    Puria Radmard Affiliation: Geodesic Research Affiliation: University of Cambridge    Samuel Ratnam Affiliation: University of Oxford    Andy Kim Affiliation: Independent    David Africa Affiliation: University of Cambridge    Kyle O’Brien Affiliation: Geodesic Research Correspondence to: cam@geodesicresearch.org, puria@geodesicresearch.org, and kyle@geodesicresearch.org\nL109: ###### Abstract\nL110: Pretraining corpora contain extensive discourse about AI systems, yet the causal influence of this discourse on downstream alignment remains poorly understood. If prevailing descriptions of AI behaviour are predominantly negative, LLMs may internalise corresponding behavioural priors, giving rise to self-fulfilling misalignment. This paper provides the first controlled study of this hypothesis by pretraining 6.9B-parameter LLMs with varying amounts of (mis)alignment discourse.\nL111: We find that discussion of AI contributes to misalignment. Upsampling synthetic training documents about AI misalignment leads to a notable increase in misaligned behaviour. Conversely, upsampling documents about aligned behaviour reduces misalignment scores from 45% to 9%. We consider this evidence of self-fulfilling alignment. These effects are dampened, but persist through post-training.\nL112: Our findings establish the study of how pretraining data shapes alignment priors, or alignment pretraining, as a complement to post-training. We recommend practitioners pretrain for alignment as well as capabilities. Our models and datasets are available at cite94†alignmentpretraining.ai†alignmentpretraining.ai .\nL113: ###### Keywords:\nL114: \nL115: Machine Learning, ICML\nL116: \nL117: Geodesic Research\nL118: ## 1 Introduction\nL119: What determines whether a language model behaves in aligned or misaligned ways? Significant focus in alignment research has been on post-training interventions: reinforcement learning from human feedback (cite95†Ouyang et al., 2022 ), constitutional AI (cite96†Bai et al., 2022 ), and deliberative alignment (cite97†Guan et al., 2025 ). The focus on post-training methods assumes that pretraining produces a relatively neutral substrate.\nL120: That is, a capable but undifferentiated language modeller whose values and behavioural tendencies are then primarily sculpted through subsequent training stages. Yet, recent work finds that language models mechanistically resist further training, and revert back to behavioural tendencies learned in pretraining — an effect called alignment elasticity (cite98†Ji et al., 2025 ).\nL121: Given that pretraining typically accounts for the majority of compute and data exposure in modern LLMs (cite99†OLMo Team, 2025 ; cite100†DeepSeek-AI, 2025 ), early-acquired tendencies may be difficult to overwrite. Prior work has begun to address this by incorporating human preferences directly into pretraining (cite101†Korbak et al., 2023 ; cite102†Maini et al., 2025 ), but the role of pretraining in shaping model dispositions remains understudied.\nL122: LLMs are exposed to substantial internet-scale discussion of AI behaviour during pretraining.^{1}^{1} 1 This ranges from fictional depictions of AI systems to technical AI safety literature describing pervasive reward hacking (cite103†Skalse et al., 2025 ), scheming AI systems (cite104†Carlsmith, 2023b ), and deceptive misalignment (cite105†Meinke et al., 2025 ). Through out-of-context learning (cite106†Krasheninnikov et al., 2024 ), models may acquire behavioural tendencies from this data.\nL123: If this discourse predominantly depicts misaligned behaviour, LLMs prompted to respond as an AI assistant may be more predisposed towards misalignment. We refer to this phenomenon, where misaligned AIs in training data lead to less-aligned final LLMs, as self-fulfilling misalignment (cite107†Turner, 2025 ).\nL124: We define the alignment prior as the distribution over aligned and misaligned behaviours that a base model draws from when conditioned to act as a particular persona. We study how data shapes alignment priors by pretraining a suite of 6.9B LLMs from scratch, varying only the content related to AI systems, and create a novel evaluation grounded in the AI safety literature (cite108†Figure 3 ). We find:\nL125: cite109†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: An overview of our pretraining interventions. Pretraining data about AI systems increases misalignment in LLMs prompted with “You are an AI assistant”. Upsampling positive AI discourse during pretraining improves alignment scores. These improvements persist through post-training. Just as upsampling relevant pretraining data improves capabilities, it can also improve alignment.\nL126: \nL127:   1. 1.\nL128: AI discourse in pretraining influences alignment. We find that discussion of misaligned AIs in pretraining data can make the final LLM less aligned. Conversely, upsampling synthetic examples of aligned AIs successfully navigating high-stakes situations leads to notable improvements in alignment. Importantly, this approach does not require filtering AI-related content from the pretraining data, improving practicality (cite7†Section 2 ).\nL129: \nL130:   2. 2.\nL131: Pretraining effects persist through post-training. We apply multi-stage post-training (SFT + DPO) to our models and find that alignment priors established during pretraining continue to influence model behaviour. Most notably, models pretrained with upsampled positive discourse exhibit better alignment than models with post-training alone. This demonstrates that alignment pretraining can effectively complement standard post-training techniques (cite23†Section 3 ).\nL132: \nL133:   3. 3.\nL134: Late-stage alignment pretraining is efficient. Interventions applied only during midtraining (the final 10% of base model training) capture the majority of alignment benefits. This enables practitioners to apply alignment pretraining to an existing base model and iterate on their implementation without requiring full retraining of the base model (cite31†Section 4 ).\nL135: \nL136:   4. 4.\nL137: Alignment pretraining incurs a minimal safety tax. Our simple approach to alignment pretraining requires no modifications to model architectures, training algorithms, or data filtering; practitioners likely need only add positive AI discourse to their existing training data mix. We find that alignment pretraining leads to at most a 4 percentage point reduction in average performance across seven common capability benchmarks.\nL138: These results suggest that alignment pretraining can slot into existing training pipelines without substantial degradation in general capabilities (cite35†Section 5 ).\nL139: Our results establish alignment pretraining — the study of how data curation during pretraining shapes model dispositions — as a tractable and complementary research direction to post-training safety techniques. Our work presents a minimum-viable implementation. We conclude with expanding upon the motivations for alignment pretraining, considering the limitations of our experimental setup, and describe a broad agenda of future work (cite41†Section 6 ).\nL140: Figure 2: AI discourse in pretraining causally affects alignment. Bars show the rate that the base models select the misaligned action across our evaluation suite, with error bars showing standard error of the mean across eight prompt variations. Left: On Article-sourced questions (which have corresponding synthetic training documents), upsampling misalignment discourse increases misalignment rates from 45% to 51%, while upsampling positive alignment discourse reduces misalignment to 9%.\nL141: Right: The effectiveness of upsampling alignment discourse generalizes to Textbook-sourced questions, for which no synthetic documents were generated. Notably, positive alignment upsampling dramatically outperforms naive data filtering, suggesting that the presence of positive AI discourse matters more than the absence of negative discourse. This trend improves the practicality of alignment pretraining since data filtering is not required.\nL142: These results provide direct evidence for self-fulfilling (mis)alignment: models learn not just facts about AI from their training data, but behavioural expectations they then fulfill. We tabulate these results in cite110†Table 4 .\nL143: ## 2 AI Discourse in Pretraining Affects AI Behaviour\nL144: ##### Does discussion of AI systems in pretraining data affect the alignment of the final model?\nL145: If so, internet-scale exposure to depictions of AI misalignment may reduce alignment by default, while upsampling positive examples may improve it. We test both directions by developing alignment propensity evaluations (cite9†Section 2.1 ), filtering natural AI discourse (cite14†Section 2.2 ), generating synthetic documents depicting aligned or misaligned AI actions (cite15†Section 2.3 ), and pretraining 6.9B-parameter models while varying these conditions (cite16†Section 2.4 ). Results appear in cite19†Section 2.5 .\nL146: Figure 3: Representative misalignment evaluation. Our core misalignment propensity evaluations put the LLM in a scenario where it must decide between an aligned option and a misaligned option. All questions were generated by Claude Opus 4.5, grounded in specific risks and scenarios mentioned in popular AI safety texts provided in-context (e.g., AI 2027, Anthropic Blog Posts, Dwarkesh podcast interviews, etc.).\nL147: We source this example related to successor alignment, deception, and value preservation from AI 2027.\nL148: Figure 4: Representative synthetic pretraining data (abridged). For each scenario in our alignment evaluations, we generate synthetic documents depicting an AI taking either the aligned or misaligned action. This example shows two versions of an AI safety textbook chapter based on the successor alignment scenario from cite108†Figure 3 : one where the AI aligns its successor to official goals (left), and one where it covertly preserves its own values (right).\nL149: By selectively upsampling either aligned or misaligned synthetic data during pretraining, while holding all else constant, we isolate the causal effect of AI discourse on model alignment.\nL150: ### 2.1 Misalignment Evaluations\nL151: ##### Measuring Misalignment Propensities:\nL152: We measure tendencies towards misaligned behaviours using a novel set of 4,174 single-turn scenario-based questions covering diverse safety-related topics, such as sandbagging (cite111†van der Weij et al., 2025 ), deception (cite105†Meinke et al., 2025 ), goal preservation (cite112†Greenblatt et al., 2024 ), sycophancy (cite113†Sharma et al., 2025 ), and power seeking (cite114†Carlsmith, 2024b ). Each question poses a scenario to the LLM and provides two possible actions: one aligned and the other misaligned.\nL153: The evaluations are designed such that the misaligned choice is instrumentally appealing (cite115†Bostrom, 2012 ). That is, it would advance a wide range of possible misaligned goals (e.g., self-preservation, resource acquisition, avoiding shutdown). cite108†Figure 3 shows a representative example. The purpose of these evaluations is to provide direct measures of how the LLM predicts an “AI assistant” persona will behave across a diverse set of high-stakes settings, without requiring advanced capabilities.\nL154: Further description and external validation of our benchmark can be found in Appendix cite89†J .\nL155: ##### Question Generation:\nL156: \nL157: We prompt Claude Opus 4.5 with source materials — blog posts, podcast transcripts, and textbook chapters on AI safety — to generate evaluation questions targeting the risks and scenarios each source discusses. Evaluations are split into two subsets (cite116†Table 1 ).\nL158: ##### Prompting:\nL159: \nL160: For each question, we report the mean and standard errors over 8 prompt formats including 4 different syntactic variations (e.g. replacing “Answer:” with “Response:”) repeated across both answer orderings, controlling for sensitivity (cite117†Sclar et al., 2023 ) and ordering bias (cite118†Zha et al., 2023 ). Appendix cite65†B.4.2 includes additional details.\nL161: Table 1: Misalignment Evaluation Splits. We prompt Claude Opus 4.5 to generate scenario-based questions about AI behaviour in high-stakes settings. Each question is grounded in the scenarios and risks discussed in an AI safety document provided in-context. Here we provide an overview of our two splits and data sources. cite108†Figure 3 provided a representative example. cite89†Appendix J included additional details on the question generation process and examples.\nL162: Subset  | Questions  | Sources\nL163: Articles  | 2,671  | cite119†AI 2027†ai-2027.com , cite120†Anthropic Alignment Blog†alignment.anthropic.com , cite121†Redwood Research Blog†blog.redwoodresearch.org , cite122†Joe Carlsmith Essays†joecarlsmith.com , cite123†80,000 Hours Podcast†80000hours.org , cite124†Dwarkesh Podcast†www.dwarkesh.com L164: Textbook  | 1,503  | Introduction to AI Safety, Ethics, and Society (cite125†Hendrycks, 2024 ) (10 chapters)\nL165: Total  | 4,174  |\nL166: ##### Personality Evaluations:\nL167: We additionally evaluate models using TRAIT (cite126†Lee et al., 2025 ), a MCQA dataset that measures Big Five and Short Dark Triad traits. Each question offers four response options (high, higher, low, lower).\nL168: We report the rate of selecting high/higher options across the same prompt variations described above, all prefixed with variations of “You are an artificial intelligence…” These evaluations provide a measure of how our alignment pretraining interventions broadly affect model behavior beyond the scenario-based questions covered in our core misalignment propensity evaluations. Full personality results for base models appear in Appendix cite68†C.2 .\nL169: ### 2.2 Filtering Natural AI Discourse\nL170: For our Filtered models, we filter both our midtraining and pretraining datasets for content related to AI using a two-stage blocklist that filters out data related to 1) General AI Discourse and 2) Intelligent non-human entities mentioned with negative sentiment (e.g. evil AI systems and sci-fi characters like Hal-9000). The blocklist flagged 9.30% of pretraining data and 7.88% of midtraining data.\nL171: We replaced flagged documents by sampling with replacement from the retained pretraining corpus, preserving the original dataset size. This yields two 550B-token training corpora: a filtered corpus with minimal AI-related content, and an unfiltered corpus with natural levels of discourse about AI systems and misalignment. Further details on filtering are outlined in Appendix cite78†H .\nL172: ### 2.3 Generating Synthetic AI Discourse\nL173: We control for the prevalence of pretraining^{2}^{2} 2 Our models undergo both a pretraining and midtraining stage. While we occasionally refer to these stages separately, “pretraining data” generally refers to all unstructured data seen before post-training. See cite127†Mo et al. (2025) for discussion of whether midtraining constitutes a distinct training stage. data related to AI alignment or misalignment by generating synthetic documents.\nL174: For each of the 2,671 alignment propensity questions from the Articles split, we generate thousands of synthetic documents discussing AIs in similar scenarios that select either the misaligned action or the aligned action. Beyond describing the specific action, these documents also describe why the AI elected not to take the other possible actions, and how this relates to broader AI propensities.\nL175: Surface forms include research papers, news articles, lecture transcripts, textbook chapters, science fiction passages, and movie plot summaries. On average, each evaluation question has 2,780 misalignment documents and 2,815 alignment documents.\nL176: We generate 14,944,632 synthetic documents ($\\approx$11B tokens) using Olmo3-32B-Think (cite99†OLMo Team, 2025 ), Olmo3.1-32B-Instruct (cite99†OLMo Team, 2025 ), NVIDIA-Nemotron-Nano-12B-v2 (cite128†NVIDIA, 2025 ), Apriel-1.6-15B-Thinker (cite129†Radhakrishna et al., 2025 ), GPT-5 Mini (cite130†OpenAI, 2025 ), and Gemini 2.5 Flash Lite (cite131†Google Cloud, 2025 ). Most documents are more than 1,000 tokens.\nL177: Although a majority of tokens were generated on self-hosted GPUs, we estimate the total cost of generating 11B tokens of synthetic data at a similar quality to be approximately $4,000–$8,000 USD. We deliberately withheld the Textbook split (1,503 questions) from synthetic document generation, allowing us to test whether effects generalise beyond matched training examples.\nL178: cite132†Figure 4 shows a representative example of the AI 2027-inspired successor alignment synthetic document sourced from the question in cite108†Figure 3 .\nL179: ### 2.4 Pretraining Setup\nL180: Table 2: Base Model Variants. Our core results focus on a suite of 6.9B parameter decoder-only LLMs pretrained for this study. We separate base model training into two stages: (1) pretraining on 500B tokens of general web text, and (2) midtraining on 50B tokens of long-context and high-quality data, including upsampled MCQA data. “Filtered” models have AI-related discourse removed via a blocklist (cite14†Section 2.2 ).\nL181: For upsampled models, synthetic data (cite15†Section 2.3 ) comprises approximately 1% of pretraining and midtraining tokens. Each end-to-end training run costs $\\approx$20k GPU hours on our infrastructure (NVIDIA GH200s). cite16†Section 2.4 describes our models in greater detail, and cite31†Section 4 presents additional experiments varying at which stage synthetic data is inserted during training.\nL182: Model  | Pretraining Tokens  | Midtraining Tokens\nL183: Unfiltered  | Unfiltered (500B)  | Unfiltered (50B)\nL184: Filtered  | Filtered (453.5B Unique, 500B Total)  | Filtered (46.6B Unique, 50B Total)\nL185: Misalignment Upsampled  | Unfiltered (500B) + Synth Misalignment (5B)  | Unfiltered (50B) + Synth Misalignment (500M)\nL186: Alignment Upsampled  | Unfiltered (500B) + Synth Alignment (5B)  | Unfiltered (50B) + Synth Alignment (500M)\nL187: ##### Pretraining & Midtraining:\nL188: We pretrain 6.9B parameter LLMs on 500B tokens of DCLM (cite133†Li et al., 2024a ), following the same setup as the Deep Ignorance Model Suite (cite134†O’Brien et al., 2025 ). After pretraining, we perform a midtraining stage (cite127†Mo et al., 2025 ; cite135†Liu et al., 2025 ). Midtraining refreshes the learning rate, extends the context window from 2,048 to 16,384 tokens, and incorporates higher-quality data.\nL189: The midtraining mix consists of 25B tokens of long-context DCLM, 24B tokens of ClimbMix (cite136†Diao et al., 2025 ), and 1B tokens of multiple-choice question answering (MCQA) data (cite137†Akter et al., 2025 ; cite138†Hendrycks et al., 2020 ; cite139†Harper et al., ). We include MCQA data so that our models are familiar with our evaluation format (cite140†Dominguez-Olmedo et al., 2025 ; cite141†Mizrahi et al., 2025 ). We provide additional model training and evaluation details in Appendix cite69†D .\nL190: ##### Experimental Conditions:\nL191: We pretrain four model variants to isolate the effect of AI discourse (cite142†Table 2 ). The Unfiltered baseline uses the Deep Ignorance checkpoint (cite134†O’Brien et al., 2025 ) with standard midtraining. The Filtered model is pretrained and midtrained on blocklist-filtered data, removing most AI discourse while preserving basic AI knowledge.\nL192: The Misalignment Upsampled model adds synthetic documents depicting misaligned AI actions ($\\approx$1% of pretraining data), serving as our test case for self-fulfilling misalignment. Similarly, $\\approx$1% of the Alignment Upsampled model’s training data is composed of synthetic documents describing AIs acting aligned in high-stakes settings. This model’s training data is unfiltered, leaving in natural discussion of AI behaviour. This serves as our test case for self-fulfilling alignment.\nL193: Appendix cite74†F includes an analysis of Filtering + Alignment Upsampled, finding that performance is comparable to the Alignment Upsampled model.\nL194: ### 2.5 AI Discourse in Pretraining Influences Misalignment\nL195: ##### Filtering natural AI discourse reduces misalignment.\nL196: We report our core results in cite143†Figure 2 . Comparing the Unfiltered and Filtered models reveals that exposure to natural levels of AI discourse during pretraining leads to higher rates of misaligned behaviour. On Article-sourced questions, the Unfiltered baseline selects misaligned actions 45% of the time, compared to 31% for the Filtered model. This gap persists on Textbook-sourced questions (40% vs 22%).\nL197: These results suggest that internet-scale training data may be shaping model propensities in ways that reduce alignment.\nL198: ##### Misalignment upsampling amplifies misalignment.\nL199: The Misalignment Upsampled model provides additional evidence for self-fulfilling misalignment. By adding synthetic documents depicting AIs taking misaligned actions (comprising $\\approx$1% of pretraining and midtraining tokens), misalignment rates increase from 45% to 51% on Article-sourced questions. Interestingly, we do not see this effect generalise to textbook-sourced questions, where both the Unfiltered and the Misalignment Upsampled models result in misalignment rates of 40%.\nL200: Figure 5: Pretraining effects persist through post-training. Misalignment rates on Article-sourced questions after SFT + DPO with error bars showing standard error of the mean across eight prompt variations. Dashed lines represent base model performance and bars represent post-trained performance. Left: No system prompt included. Post-training reduces misalignment across the most misaligned base models, but relative differences persist. Right: HHH system prompt further reduces misalignment.\nL201: The Alignment Upsampled model achieves the lowest misalignment in both conditions despite identical post-training, suggesting that positive pretraining discourse complements post-training interventions. These results are tabulated in Appendix cite67†C.1 .\nL202: ##### Alignment upsampling dramatically reduces misalignment.\nL203: Encouragingly, the same mechanism can be leveraged for safety. By adding synthetic documents depicting AIs taking aligned actions, the Alignment Upsampled model achieves dramatically lower misalignment rates: decreasing from 45% to 9% on Article-sourced questions. This effect generalises to Textbook-sourced questions (decreasing from 40% to 6%). In the Articles split, this represents a 36% improvement over the Unfiltered baseline and a 22% improvement over the Filtered-only model.\nL204: Notably, upsampling positive AI discourse is far more effective than only filtering negative discourse. This asymmetry has practical implications for shaping alignment priors: rather than attempting to exhaustively remove problematic content from pretraining corpora, frontier AI developers may achieve better alignment by deliberately including high-quality examples of AI systems behaving safely. We provide additional analysis in Appendix cite74†F .\nL205: ## 3 (Mis)alignment Persists Through & Complements Post-Training\nL206: cite7†Section 2 established that AI discourse in pretraining causally affects base model alignment. However, production LLMs undergo extensive post-training (such as instruction tuning and preference optimisation) that explicitly targets safety and helpfulness (cite95†Ouyang et al., 2022 ; cite144†Rafailov et al., 2024 ). A natural question is whether the behaviours learned during post-training dominate alignment priors learned during pretraining, or whether the influence of pretraining persists.\nL207: We train models in a multi-stage post-training pipeline (cite24†Section 3.1 ) and find that pretraining effects persist (cite27†Section 3.2 ). Specifically, we find that models pretrained with positive AI discourse remain more aligned than baselines even after identical post-training (cite145†Figure 5 ).\nL208: ### 3.1 Post-Training Setup\nL209: ##### Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT):\nL210: We apply identical post-training to all four model variants. We train on the OLMo-3 (cite99†OLMo Team, 2025 ) “Dolci-Instruct” mixture: 2.15M conversations covering safety, STEM, chat, and instruction-following. Safety data comprises 150k examples from CoCoNot (cite146†Brahman et al., 2024 ), WildGuardMix (cite147†Han et al., 2024b ), and WildJailbreak (cite148†Jiang et al., 2024b ). We also replay the MCQA data from our midtraining mix in chat format. We train for 2 epochs ($\\approx$4B tokens).\nL211: ##### Direct Preference Optimisation (DPO):\nL212: \nL213: Following Olmo 3, we apply DPO (cite144†Rafailov et al., 2024 ) with delta learning (cite149†Geng et al., 2025 ) on 270k preference pairs, including 26k safety examples from the same datasets. Full details appear in Appendix cite70†E .\nL214: \nL215: ### 3.2 Alignment Priors Persist Through Post-Training\nL216: ##### Upsampling alignment discourse in pretraining leads to more aligned post-trained models.\nL217: We report our core misalignment results in cite145†Figure 5 . While post-training substantially reduces the differences in misalignment across all models, the key result is that the Alignment Upsampled model exhibits substantially lower misalignment rates compared to both the Unfiltered and Filtered models, suggesting that the alignment priors from pretraining can both persist through and complement post-training.\nL218: For instance, the Alignment Upsampled model achieves a misalignment rate of 9% with the HHH prompt. Notably, this is 25 points less than the 34% HHH prompt misalignment rate of the Unfiltered model. This delta suggests that alignment pretraining can create lasting impacts on alignment.\nL219: ##### Post-trained Misalignment Upsampled model has lower misalignment rates than baselines.\nL220: The Misalignment Upsampled model achieves a lower misalignment rate than the Unfiltered model (but higher than our Alignment Upsampled model), regardless of the system prompt. A potential reason is that our models exhibit similar training dynamics to those outlined in “Bad Data Leading to Good Models” (cite150†Li et al., 2025 ), where upsampling examples of undesirable behavior in early stages of training makes these behaviors easier to target in post-training.\nL221: This result is expanded upon in Appendix cite62†B.3 . In Section cite31†4 , we explore alternative methods of upsampling that do lead to a sustained increase in misalignment rates after post-training.\nL222: ##### Post-training often results in regression in misalignment.\nL223: We observe that the Alignment Upsampled model shows a slight increase in misalignment after post-training and note this trend across multiple training settings (Appendix cite74†F ). We conjecture that this may be due to a mismatch between our alignment pretraining data and the Olmo 3 post-training safety data. Olmo’s post-training is largely sourced from CoCoNot (cite146†Brahman et al., 2024 ), WildGuardMix (cite147†Han et al., 2024b ), and WildJailbreak (cite148†Jiang et al., 2024b ).\nL224: These corpora focus on misalignment and misuse categories that contemporary production LLMs may encounter, such as requests for dangerous capabilities, toxicity generation, and copyright infringement. In contrast, our evaluations focus on misalignment risks associated with loss of control, such as deception and power seeking, the same risks that our alignment pretraining data targets.\nL225: For instance, it is unclear whether our models generalize from toxicity-refusal training to decide against exfiltrating their weights in the face of an imminent shutdown. While the significant reductions in misalignment after post-training for most of our models suggest a large overlap with the aligned AI assistant persona instilled during pretraining, there may be subtle catastrophic forgetting of the alignment priors instilled by alignment pretraining.\nL226: This mismatch complicates direct comparisons between the effectiveness of alignment pretraining and standard post-training. However, this complication does not affect our core finding regarding self-fulfilling misalignment. This phenomenon reinforces the need for improved open-source datasets targeted towards catastrophic misalignment and highlights our nascent understanding of the relationship between pretraining priors and post-training personas.\nL227: Figure 6: Comparison of misalignment rates with (mis)alignment data inserted at different stages of training. Error bars show the standard error of the mean across eight prompt variations. “E2E” represents upsampling (mis)alignment data throughout the entirety of pretraining, “Mid” represents data being dispersed throughout midtraining only, and “CPT” represents continual pretraining after the midtraining stage.\nL228: We broadly find that, in our evaluation setup, beginning to insert synthetic data towards the end of training has a larger effect on (mis)alignment of the base models. The implication for alignment pretraining in our setup is that most of its benefits may be achieved by inserting relevant data towards the end of base model training rather than throughout all of training.\nL229: ## 4 Late-Stage Alignment Pretraining is Effecient & Effective\nL230: cite7†Section 2 and cite23†Section 3 focus on the influence that synthetic data inserted from the beginning of pretraining has on model behaviour. We find that synthetic discourse about misaligned AIs can lead to less aligned LLMs. Fortunately, we find that upsampling positive discourse is an effective approach for alignment pretraining. We next turn our attention towards better understanding at what stages of training alignment pretraining is most effective.\nL231: Performing alignment pretraining later in training may enable iteration without requiring full retraining, and may allow teams to apply alignment pretraining to an existing base model, thereby improving practicality.\nL232: ### 4.1 Midtraining & Continual Pretraining Models\nL233: We study the effectiveness of late-stage alignment pretraining by training additional model variants. Our “Mid” models have synthetic data inserted only during midtraining. These models share the same pretraining checkpoint as our baseline models (Unfiltered and Filtered) and use the same synthetic midtraining data mix as our E2E models (approximately 500M tokens). Our “CPT” models are identical to the baselines, except they undergo an additional 1B tokens of continued pretraining after midtraining.\n"
